\details{
\code{ImportSS()} reads SS3 output files from \code{SSDir} and constructs an
\code{\link[=OM]{OM()}} object. Biological processes, fleet structure, and historical time series
are inferred directly from the SS3 report output and mapped into the
corresponding OM slots.
\itemize{
\item Multiple simulation replicates are supported when multiple SS3 runs are detected.
\item Optional metadata describing authorship, region, and stock identity are stored in the resulting OM.
\item By default, the OM is fully populated using \code{PopulateOM()}.
\item Set \code{Populate = FALSE} to return the partially constructed OM.
\item All stochastic values (e.g., projection recruitment deviations,
observation and implementation error) are generated from \code{Seed}, so OMs
imported with the same \code{Seed} share the same random draws. Performance
averaged across such OMs is based on a single set of \code{nSim} random draws.
Use a different \code{Seed} for each OM, or for each batch of OMs, to obtain
independent draws. Simulation \code{i} is the same for any \code{nSim}.
\item The numbers-at-age in the first historical year are the SS3 virgin
numbers-at-age multiplied by \code{SRR@RecDevInit} and
\code{Depletion@Misc$InitialAtAge}. \code{SRR@RecDevInit} holds the SS3 initial-age
recruitment deviations, scaled by any recruitment offset SS3 applies to
the initial equilibrium without fishing (e.g. a regime parameter).
\code{Depletion@Misc$InitialAtAge} holds the remaining age-specific depletion of
the initial numbers-at-age, e.g. from initial equilibrium fishing
mortality. The dynamic unfished conditions start from the virgin
numbers-at-age multiplied by \code{SRR@RecDevInit} only.
\item SS3 recruitment deviations are penalized estimates, shrunk toward zero
by an amount that increases with their SE, so their standard deviation
underestimates the recruitment variability. \code{SRR@SD} is
\code{sqrt(var(dev) + mean(SE^2))} (Methot and Taylor 2011), calculated from
the estimated Main-era recruitment deviations and their SEs
(\code{Parm_StDev}). This is the \code{alternative_sigma_R} that \code{r4ss} reports in
\code{sigma_R_info} for the Main period. If the SEs are not available (e.g.
no Hessian), they are approximated from the SS3 bias adjustment,
\code{SE^2 = sigma_R_in^2 * (1 - biasadjuster)}. If neither is available,
\code{SRR@SD} is the SS3 input \code{sigma_R} (\code{sigma_R_in}). A warning is given
when the SEs are not available.
\code{SRR@AC} is the lag-1 autocorrelation of the recruitment deviations in
the years with full bias adjustment. Shrinkage can inflate this
autocorrelation.
\item The CVs of the imported data (\code{Data@Landings@CV}, \code{Data@Discards@CV},
\code{Data@CPUE@CV}, \code{Data@Survey@CV}) are calculated from the error SS3 uses
in the fit. A log-scale SE is converted to a CV with
\code{sqrt(exp(SE^2) - 1)}, and a natural-scale SD is divided by the
observation. Catch: the log-scale \code{se} (\code{CATCH} report). Indices: the
\code{SE} (\code{INDEX_2} report; input SE plus any variance adjustment and
estimated extra SD), log-scale for lognormal and Student's t errors and
natural-scale for normal errors. Discards: the SD used in the fit
(\code{Std_use}, \code{DISCARD_OUTPUT} report), log-scale for lognormal errors
and natural-scale otherwise. CVs are \code{NA} where SS3 gives no SE (missing
or \code{0}) and for observations excluded from the discard likelihood.
